.TH ASCII85 1 "August 2026" "ascii85 2.2.0-a85x" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ascii85 \- encode and decode Ascii85 or A85X1 data
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ascii85
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.RI [ FILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ascii85
encodes FILE, or standard input, as classic Ascii85 by default.
Use
.B --decode
to decode and
.B --a85x
to select the canonical A85X1 format.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -d , " --decode"
Decode instead of encode.
.TP
.BR -x , " --a85x"
Use A85X1 instead of classic Ascii85.
.TP
.BR -i , " --ignore-garbage"
Ascii85 decode only: skip invalid non-whitespace characters.
.TP
.BR -n , " --no-delims"
Ascii85 only: omit delimiters while encoding, or do not require them while decoding.
.TP
.BR -w , " --wrap=" COLS
Ascii85 encode only: wrap encoded output after COLS characters. Use 0 to disable wrapping.
.TP
.BR -y , " --y-abbr"
Ascii85 only: abbreviate four spaces as y.
.TP
.B --version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Print help and exit.
.SH A85X1
A85X1 uses the canonical envelope
.PP
.nf
A85X1:<payload>:<pad>:<CRC32>
.fi
.PP
The CRC-32 detects accidental corruption only. It is not cryptographic authentication.
A85X1 decoding is strict and rejects trailing bytes, whitespace, malformed padding, out-of-range Base85 groups, and checksum mismatches.
Decoded output is not released to standard output until checksum verification succeeds.
.SH EXAMPLES
Encode classic Ascii85:
.PP
.nf
printf 'hello world' | ascii85
.fi
.PP
Encode A85X1:
.PP
.nf
printf 'hello world' | ascii85 --a85x
.fi
.PP
Decode A85X1:
.PP
.nf
printf 'A85X1:Xk~0_Zy.MXa%[M(:1:0D4A1185' | ascii85 -dx
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns zero on success and nonzero for malformed input, invalid options, I/O failures, or A85X checksum failure.
.SH SECURITY
A85X1 CRC-32 is not a MAC or signature. Do not use it to authenticate hostile data.
